    "lawCode": "Add Art 8 Title 9-A §§1890 - 1899-a, amd §1855, Pub Auth L; add §99-g, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5888               REVISED 09/10/09\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to\nestablishing the \"green jobs-green New York act of 2009\"; and to amend\nthe state finance law, in relation to establishing the green\njobs-green New York fund\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nEstablish a \"Green Jobs/Green New York\" program administered by the\nNew York State Energy Research and Development Authority (NYSERDA)\nwith a goal of making 1,000,000 homes, businesses, and not-for-profits\nin New York more energy efficient while creating thousands of new\ngreen jobs and training the workers to fill them.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection one:\n\n*Provides definitions for the Green Jobs/Green New York program.\n\n*Authorizes NYSERDA to establish the Green Jobs/Green New York Program\nby implementing a demonstration program to provide funding for the\nperformance of energy audits and energy-efficient retrofits for\nresidential, small business, and not-for-profit property owners in\norder to reduce dependence on fossil fuels, lower housing costs,\nsupport community development and create green jobs to sustain and\nenhance our economy.\n\n*Requires NYSERDA to establish innovative financing mechanisms such as\non-bill financing that allow consumers to pay for these improvements\nthrough energy savings over a period of time.\n\n*Requires: selection of target communities for the program through a\ncompetitive process and requires applicants to include local community\ngroups in collaboration with contractors, local utilities, including\nLIPA and NYPA and labor and training organizations; geographic\ndiversity; and, criteria for awards to include preferences to\ncoalitions that include women and minority based businesses and groups\nbased in economically distressed communities.\n\n*Provides $112 million of funding from the Regional Greenhouse Gas\nInitiative (RGGI)\n\n*Establishes a Green Jobs/Green New York advisory council to advise\nNYSERDA on the creation and implementation of the program.\n\n*Requires NYSERDA to submit an annual report to the Governor, the\nTemporary President of the Senate, the Speaker of the Assembly, the\nMinority Leader of the Senate and the Minority Leader of the Assembly\nconcerning the authorities activities related to the Green Jobs- Green\nNew York program.\n\nSection 2 requires NYSERDA to establish rules and regulations to\ngovern the program.\n\nSection 3 requires DEC to deposit $112 million of RGGI funds into the\nGreen Jobs/Green New York fund.\n\nSection 4 establishes the Green Jobs/Green New York fund in the joint\ncustody of the Comptroller and the Commissioner of Taxation and\nFinance.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nPromoting widespread dissemination of energy conservation and clean\nenergy technologies represents a clear and cost-effective pathway for\ncommunities in New York State to curtail emissions of greenhouse gases\nand harmful air contaminants, reduce dependence on fossil fuels, lower\nhousing costs, support community development and create green jobs to\nsustain and enhance our economy. Installation of energy efficiency\nimprovements to reduce the loss or waste of energy is a proven method\nthat can pay for itself over reasonable time periods.  However, lack\nof affordable and accessible financing for many owners of residential\nproperties, small businesses and non-profit organizations has hindered\nprogress in fully realizing the promise of these technologies. By\nenacting a \"Green Jobs/Green New York\" program, the state will\nauthorize the performance of energy-efficient retrofits at no initial\ncost to residential property owners, with an ultimate goal of\nimproving at least one million residential units over the next five\nyears, and demonstrate the effectiveness of innovative financing\nmechanisms in reaching this goal.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2009: New\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n$112 million of funding from the Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative\n(RGGI)\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
